' bug-668: a user type whose BARE name equals a builtin package's override type
' must not be routed to that package's renderer.
'
' `toString` is overridable, and `color`, `net` and `vector` each register an
' override for one of their own value types (`color::Color`, `net::Url`,
' `vector::Float2`). The override table was keyed by the type's BARE spelling, so
' a user `TYPE Color` matched the `color` row: the call type-checked and then the
' build died with `NIR call target '#color_toString' does not resolve`, because
' the program never imported `color`.
'
' A package override applies to that package's OWN type only. A user type the
' built-in rejects is a `TYPE_CALL_ARGUMENT_MISMATCH` at the call, exactly as a
' `TYPE Colour` already was.
'
' The partner assertions — the overrides themselves still fire for the packages'
' own types — are `tests/rt-behavior/color/color_to_string_rt` and the `toString`
' calls in `tests/byte-identity/vector`.

TYPE Color
  r AS Integer
END TYPE

TYPE Url
  href AS String
END TYPE

TYPE Float2
  x AS Float
  y AS Float
END TYPE

FUNC main AS Integer
  LET c AS Color = Color[1]
  LET u AS Url = Url["https://example.com"]
  LET v AS Float2 = Float2[1.0, 2.0]
  LET colorText AS String = toString(c)
  LET urlText AS String = toString(u)
  LET vectorText AS String = toString(v)
  RETURN 0
END FUNC
